ob": 0.9897487759590149, "span": {"begin": 1054, "end": 1064}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3240"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "haptoglobin", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9899258613586426, "span": {"begin": 1066, "end": 1077}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:325"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "serum amyloid P", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9842215776443481, "span": {"begin": 1079, "end": 1094}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:7018"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "transferrin", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9916173815727234, "span": {"begin": 1100, "end": 1111}}, {"id": ["NCBITaxon:10095"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "mice", "obj": "species", "prob": 0.9840030074119568, "span": {"begin": 1129, "end": 1133}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6287"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "SAA", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.991592526435852, "span": {"begin": 1234, "end": 1237}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:3240"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "haptoglobin", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.990943968296051, "span": {"begin": 1239, "end": 1250}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:6288"], "is_neural_normalized": true, "mention": "serum amyloid", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.561626136302948, "span": {"begin": 1255, "end": 1268}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1508"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "APPs", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.546838104724884, "span": {"begin": 1307, "end": 1311}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "blood", "obj": "cell_type", "prob": 0.5726251602172852, "span": {"begin": 1332, "end": 1337}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:1508"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "APPs", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9779583811759949, "span": {"begin": 1372, "end": 1376}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "mRNA", "obj": "RNA", "prob": 0.8804176449775696, "span": {"begin": 1401, "end": 1405}}, {"id": ["CUI-less"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "acute phase genes", "obj": "DNA", "prob": 0.75520920753479, "span": {"begin": 1416, "end": 1433}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:351"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "APP", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9878925085067749, "span": {"begin": 1474, "end": 1477}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:351"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "APP", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.7052400708198547, "span": {"begin": 1569, "end": 1572}}, {"id": ["NCBIGene:351"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "APP", "obj": "gene", "prob": 0.9962233304977417, "span": {"begin": 1666, "end": 1669}}, {"id": ["mesh:D005334"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "fever", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.7416388988494873, "span": {"begin": 1783, "end": 1788}}, {"id": ["mesh:D007964"], "is_neural_normalized": false, "mention": "leucocytosis", "obj": "disease", "prob": 0.9960987567901611, "span": {"begin": 1793, "end": 1805}}], "text": "Acute phase response (APR) is the systemic response to acute and chronic inflammatory states, for example bacterial infection, trauma and infarction. Acute phase proteins (APP) have been shown to be valuable biomarkers as increase can occur with inflammation, infection, neoplasia, stress, and trauma (Cray, 2012). Proteins are considered APP when their plasma concentration changes at least 25% (up or down) in response to these stressors &nbsp;(Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). All animals--from fish to mammals--have APP, but the type of major APP differs by species (Cray, 2012).   APPs that increase in concentration during APR are called positive APP, while negative APP are decreased during APR (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). In humans, the two major positive APPs are C-reactive protein (CRP) and serum amyloid A (SAA), whose concentration can increase more than 1000-fold during an acute phase response (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). Additional human acute phase proteins include &nbsp;Alpha-1 antitrypsin, albumin, alpha-2 macroglobulin, ceruloplasmin, fibrinogen, haptoglobin, serum amyloid P, and transferrin (Cray, 2012). In mice, the major positive APPs (i.e. the APPs whose plasma concentration changes the most during APR) are SAA, haptoglobin and serum amyloid P (Cray, Zaias, &amp; Altman, 2009).  APPs are detected in the blood. The organ of biosynthesis of the APPs may vary, and increased mRNA levels of acute phase genes in organ tissue may provide evidence of APP expression in a given organ. Traditionally, the liver has been regarded as a major site of APP biosynthesis (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999). However, other organs such as the lung may express APP following exposure to stressors (Saber et al., 2013). In addition, acute phase response is also characterized by fever and leucocytosis (Gabay &amp; Kushner, 1999).", "timestamp": "Tue Oct 13 15:40:22 +0000 2026"}